@node Installation
@section Installation
@cindex installation

Stable builds are published to the platform stores. Beta builds and
command-line artefacts live on GitHub Releases.

If you build from source, see @ref{Building from Source}.

@node First Run
@section First Run

On first launch SotF asks for a music library root. Point it at a
directory of FLAC/MP3/WAV/AIFF/Opus files; the indexer scans in the
background. You can:

@itemize @bullet
@item Play a track to confirm the audio device works.
@item Open the @samp{Plugins} screen to add an EQ in the chain.
@item Open @samp{Headphone EQ} or @samp{Room EQ} to design filters.
@end itemize

@node The Desktop Player
@section The Desktop Player

@cindex GPUI player
@cindex desktop player

The desktop player (binary @code{sotf-desktop}, crate @code{app-gpui})
is built on GPUI. It exposes:

@table @asis
@item Library
Album / artist / track browsing with sort and filter.
@item Queue
Drag-to-reorder play queue with persistence.
@item Plugins
Per-track or global plugin rack/graph editor.
@item Spectrum / Loudness
Real-time analysers wired to the audio engine taps.
@item Headphone EQ, Room EQ
Wizards that drive the optimiser and write filters into the chain.
@end table

@node The TUI Player
@section The TUI Player
@cindex TUI player

The TUI (@code{sotf-tui}, crate @code{app-tui}) gives the same
functionality from a terminal. It is the recommended target for
remote, headless or low-latency setups. Default keybindings are
listed in the project's reference docs.

@node Headphone EQ Workflow
@section Headphone EQ Workflow

@enumerate
@item
Pick your headphone model from the bundled measurement databases (or
load a custom CSV / REW export).
@item
Choose a target curve (Harman, custom, flat, listening profile).
@item
Run the optimiser. The result is a parametric EQ filter set plus an
optional preamp gain.
@item
Send the filters to the plugin chain or export them as
Equalizer APO @file{config.txt}.
@end enumerate

@xref{Plugin EQ}, for parameter details.

@node Speaker EQ Workflow
@section Speaker EQ Workflow

Same flow as headphones, but the input is an anechoic measurement
(CEA2034 / Spinorama-style). The optimiser respects the measurement
frequency bounds and produces filters intended for on-axis correction
plus optional preference-score tuning. @xref{Concepts on CEA2034}.

@node Room EQ Workflow
@section Room EQ Workflow

@cindex RoomEQ
@cindex room correction

Room correction is multichannel and topology-aware. SotF supports:

@itemize @bullet
@item Per-channel parametric EQ (IIR).
@item Linear-phase EQ stages.
@item Linear-phase crossovers (with phase-linear filters).
@item Per-channel delays.
@item Warped and Kautz topologies for low-frequency rooms.
@item Excess-phase / EPA temporal-masking analysis.
@end itemize

The optimiser builds a routed graph (a 9-node order with matrix-gain
folding, @code{Passthrough}-for-destination-only, and chain-override
on LFE self-routes) so the runtime cost is minimal.

@node Plugin Chains
@section Plugin Chains

The plugin host (@code{sotf-host}) accepts either:

@table @asis
@item Rack mode
A linear list of plugins with one input and one output per stage.
@item Graph mode
An explicit DAG with named nodes and per-channel routing. Useful for
multichannel processing where channels go through different chains.
@end table

A plugin is instantiated from a @code{PluginConfig}:

@example
PluginConfig @{
    plugin_type: "EQ".into(),
    parameters: json!(@{
        "filters": [@{
            "filter_type": "peak",
            "frequency": 1000.0,
            "q": 1.5,
            "gain_db": 3.0
        @}]
    @})
@}
@end example

Channel count can change between stages (e.g.@: a stereo upmixer
expands 2 to 5 channels).

@node Recording for Measurement
@section Recording for Measurement

The recorder produces multichannel WAVs suitable for AutoEQ/RoomEQ.
See the @code{recording} CLI guide and the @samp{Recording} screen.

@node macOS System-wide Audio
@section macOS System-wide Audio
@cindex macOS daemon
@cindex HAL driver

macOS does not let applications insert DSP between an output device
and the speakers. SotF ships three optional components:

@table @asis
@item @code{driver-hal}
A Core Audio HAL driver, similar in spirit to BlackHole, that exposes
a virtual device whose output is read by the daemon.
@item @code{systemwide} daemon
Reads audio from the HAL, runs a plugin chain (typically a room or
headphone EQ), and writes the processed signal back to a real device.
@item @code{sotf-macos-configbar}
A menubar app to configure the daemon and HAL driver.
@end table

This stack is currently the easiest path to true system-wide EQ on
macOS without rebooting into recovery.

@node Listening Profiles
@section Listening Profiles

A @emph{listening profile} is a saved bundle of (plugin chain +
device + target curve) that can be switched as a single action.
Profiles live in the user config directory.

@node A/B Comparison
@section A/B Comparison

The @code{ab-compare} plugin and screen let you switch between two
plugin sub-chains under controlled gain to compare them blind.

