@node Psychoacoustics Primer
@section Psychoacoustics Primer

The human auditory system is not flat. Equal-loudness contours
(ISO 226) describe how perceived loudness varies with frequency and
SPL. EQ matters because:

@itemize @bullet
@item Speakers and headphones are not flat by design or by accident.
@item Rooms add modal peaks below ~300 Hz that swamp tonal balance.
@item Critical bands set the resolution of perception (Bark/ERB).
@end itemize

@node Concepts on CEA2034
@section Concepts on CEA2034

@cindex CEA2034
@cindex Spinorama

CEA-2034 (also called Spinorama) is a standardised speaker measurement
representation: on-axis, listening window, early reflections, sound
power and the derived predicted in-room response. SotF computes the
full set in @code{autoeq-cea2034}, together with the preference
score model (Olive). The AutoEQ optimiser can target either the
on-axis curve or the preference score directly.

@node IIR vs FIR Filters
@section IIR vs FIR Filters

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{IIR} (@code{Biquad}) is cheap and minimum-phase; it
  introduces phase shifts but no pre-ringing.
@item @strong{FIR} can be designed for linear phase (zero phase
  distortion), at the cost of more CPU and an inherent latency equal
  to half its tap count.
@item Linear-phase crossovers eliminate the phase mismatch between
  ways that classical IIR crossovers introduce.
@end itemize

For room EQ, IIR is usually a better default below the Schroeder
frequency (peaks are minimum-phase by nature), while FIR may be
preferred for headphone correction where pre-ringing is masked by
the IR shape.

@node Optimisation
@section Optimisation

The optimiser lives in @code{math-de} (differential evolution, forked
from SciPy) with bridges to NLopt and a metaheuristics library. Key
points:

@itemize @bullet
@item DE is global, derivative-free, and tolerant of multimodal cost
  surfaces.
@item JADE / adaptive variants are available.
@item Linear and non-linear constraints are supported.
@item Filters must be placed within the measurement's frequency
  bounds.
@item Passband detection uses @emph{relative-to-peak} thresholds, not
  absolute dB values.
@end itemize

@node Audio Engine Architecture
@section Audio Engine Architecture
@cindex audio engine

The engine (@code{sotf-engine}) runs on a four-thread pipeline:

@enumerate
@item @strong{Decoder} thread pulls and decodes audio frames.
@item @strong{Processing} thread runs the plugin graph.
@item @strong{Playback} thread feeds the cpal callback.
@item @strong{Manager} thread coordinates state transitions.
@end enumerate

Hard rules in the audio path:

@itemize @bullet
@item Never allocate or lock in the audio callback (frame period
  ~21 ms).
@item Pre-allocate buffers in @code{build()}, reuse with
  @code{Option::take()} in @code{process()}.
@item STFT plugins must return @code{context.num_frames}, not the
  actual draining count, or the ring buffer underruns.
@item @code{NodeBuffer::clear()} only resets @code{actual_len}; it
  does not zero memory.
@item Output clipping is applied in the cpal callback to prevent
  saturation.
@end itemize

@node Room Acoustics
@section Room Acoustics

Small-room acoustics is dominated below the Schroeder frequency by
discrete modal behaviour; above it, by diffuse reflections. SotF
provides:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{math-rir} for impulse-response analysis (windowing,
  smoothing, regularisation).
@item @code{math-bem} for boundary-element solutions of simple
  geometries.
@item @code{autoeq-roomsim} for WASM-targeted in-browser simulation.
@end itemize

